1781 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 228 0 R
/T (CHAPTER 4 CONCLUSION)
/E (CHAPTER 4 CONCLUSION)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1782 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 228 0 R
/E (The Project-Based Learning development of the Hospital Appointment and Patient Care Management System \(CarePulse DBMS\) successfully demonstrates the practical implementation of relational database engineering principles applied to real-world healthcare operational bottlenecks. Beginning with formal domain analysis in Review 1, the conceptual model was structured using Classical Chen Notation across 14 core entities. During Review 2, the relational schema was decomposed rigorously up to Third Normal Form \(3NF\), eliminating all insertion, update, and deletion anomalies while enforcing domain-level business constraints through database triggers and compiled analytical views. Finally, in Review 3, a zero-dependency Python web application was developed and connected to the database engine, providing seamless clinical CRUD operations, live patient directory search, conflict-free appointment scheduling, interactive ward bed census visualization, and automated billing settlement.)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1783 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 228 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1784 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 228 0 R
/E (While the developed prototype completely fulfills the academic PBL requirements and course review milestones, the following genuine limitations exist in the current implementation, presenting promising avenues for future enterprise enhancement:)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1785 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 228 0 R
/E (1. Role-Based Cryptographic Authentication \(RBAC\): The current prototype operates on an open internal access model suited for prototype demonstration. Future releases should implement salted PBKDF2/bcrypt password hashing, multi-factor authentication \(MFA\), and JSON Web Token \(JWT\) session authorization to restrict granular CRUD permissions according to staff role.)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1786 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 228 0 R
/E (2. HL7 / FHIR Healthcare Interoperability: The current database stores standardized ICD-10 diagnostic codes but does not yet expose an HL7/FHIR \(Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources\) compliant REST API. Adding FHIR-standard JSON serialization would enable seamless electronic health record \(EHR\) data exchange with external laboratories and national health registries.)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1787 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 228 0 R
/E (3. Distributed Database Migration & High Availability: The current prototype runs on an ACID-compliant embedded SQLite engine. For high-volume multi-specialty tertiary hospitals experiencing thousands of concurrent transactions per second, the schema is)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
